      "status": "mathematical proof reconstructed",
      "correspondence": "Use the orders of χ(γ) in (ℤ/ℓ^j)^× to choose a neighbourhood forcing an approximating power γ^k to have ℓ^a∣k. Commutation then implies x^b lies in the zero fibre on U. The two coprime annihilators force φ(x)=0. The cocycle formula descends along the quotient map C×U→G.",
      "review": "Verified the open-subgroup proof without assuming its normality. Checked the finite semidirect product, both decompositions and the cocycle rule on all 54² pairs."
    },
    {
      "id": "CRC-02",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "Transporting local coboundaries and descending the extended cocycle",
      "anchor": "inputs-cyclotomic-cocycles-inflation",
      "statement": "For e≠0 the locally trivial H¹ classes for A=(ℚ_ℓ/ℤ_ℓ)(−e) correspond by inflation and restriction to equivariant locally constant characters on U vanishing on U∩I at finite places and U∩D over ℓ. A primitive a on D is transported to ρ(g)a−z(g) on gDg⁻¹. The extension cocycle factors through the quotient by the closed normal subgroup generated by inertia away from ℓ.",
      "hypotheses": "Take S the finite places over ℓ and the cyclotomic quotient G_S. The odd-prime or mod-four condition on the image supplies a topological generator. The local image over ℓ is open, e≠0, and multiplication by a nonzero scalar on ℚ_ℓ/ℤ_ℓ is surjective.",
      "definitions": [
        "A local primitive a satisfies z(d)=ρ(d)a−a for every d∈D.",
        "N is the closed normal subgroup generated by the inertia groups for v∉S, so G_S=G/N.",
        "The finite model has U=(ℤ/9)×(ℤ/2) with C∩U of order two, illustrating extension and transport."
      ],
